      "writtenOn": "2018-05-25T18:32:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I haven\u0027t come up with a test for this case yet, but one possible concern would be entry to low power mode triggered by EC reset (or some TCPC reset) and while the write to enter low power mode is ongoing (and the hook task gives up control while waiting for either I2C mutex, or waiting for the I2C write to complete), an attach event on that port which would request an exit from low power mode. In normal user actions there is no way for a port to request to enter low power mode then need that request cancelled before it takes place.",
      "parentUuid": "54b79c95_a0a4d21a",
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "258a9bec_e5520701",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 227,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-25T20:30:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was just meaning in general. Hook task is lowest priority task so anywhere through this execution path can be preempted. So if it\u0027s here at this point, but before the tcpc_write() transaction we could send the idle command which would cause state to be lost.",
      "parentUuid": "c0339e36_4efc4f37",
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c7162b8e_c20ac4b4",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 231,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-11T19:39:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I really do think that the clear should happen before tcpc_write otherwise we can\u0027t guarantee that you try to go into lower power mode (at least once) after requesting it.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 230,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 231,
        "endChar": 43
      },
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "c24a1b0c_dec38ab9",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 243,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-23T20:46:00Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Are you relying on the compiler to generate another read here of the global variable? \n\nWhat happens if we preempt here and cancel a call from the drp_toggle below? Are you relying on another port to kick this task and we eventually settle? I don\u0027t see how you guaranteed not to miss an idle request. Yes, tcpc_lpm_pending can have the request set but this hook can be canceled right here w/ a pending lpm request.",
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "4c14b9a3_3959ef27",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 243,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-25T18:32:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I made tcpc_lpm_pending volatile to make sure that its current value is always loaded. After reading your comment, I thought one potential problem would be if port 1\u0027s lpm_pending bit was set while this loop is processing port 0. I have tested with and without tcpc_lpm_pending declared as a volatile. I see no difference in behavior, but leaving volatile just to be safe.\n\nThe EC code doesn\u0027t preempt and switch tasks. This task would have to give up control for the PD task to run, which would happen either waiting for the I2C mutex, or while waiting for the tcpc_write to complete in the for loop above. The task switch can\u0027t happen between lines 243 and 244.",
      "parentUuid": "c24a1b0c_dec38ab9",
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bf894510_bdb7337c",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 243,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-25T18:56:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Doesn\u0027t an interrupt always preempt a task, which can then schedule a higher priority task in the in between any two lines of code?",
      "parentUuid": "4c14b9a3_3959ef27",
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "68665549_491a601a",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 243,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002124
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-25T20:30:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e The EC code doesn\u0027t preempt and switch tasks. This task would have\n \u003e to give up control for the PD task to run, which would happen\n \u003e either waiting for the I2C mutex, or while waiting for the\n \u003e tcpc_write to complete in the for loop above. The task switch can\u0027t\n \u003e happen between lines 243 and 244.\n\n\nI disagree. The EC is a preemptive design w.r.t. task scheduling. Why do you think a higher priority task won\u0027t preempt a lower one (or this hook task)? It definitely can be preempted here.",
      "parentUuid": "4c14b9a3_3959ef27",
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d13c1b97_eaf599f0",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 244,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-11T19:39:56Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think we should cancel a defer request in the method because we might override a request from a higher priority method. We should reschedule though.\n\nThe reschedule should be based a flag (that we set to true in an else condition on line 232).\n\nWith the these changes, we shouldn\u0027t need to make the global variables volatile",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 243,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 244,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "721657e1_f7c07cbc",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 244,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-22T00:11:17Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "If the port doesn\u0027t have a pending lpm_entry, then canceling the request is what needs to happen. Otherwise, the extra request will cause the TCPC to exit low power mode.",
      "parentUuid": "d13c1b97_eaf599f0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 243,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 244,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9ca9c62c_8e7ce39e",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 244,
      "author": {
        "id": 1259838
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-22T18:48:51Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I was looking at other code that uses atomic operation, and I saw a pattern that would alleviate any concerns I had about volatile from earlier.\n\nWe can do a atomic_read_clear to a local variable and use that local as our work queue. If we cannot complete the work, we atomic_or back on the to global and call hook_call_deferred(0). If we completed all of the work, then we don\u0027t need to clear the deferred call (with -1) since it shouldn\u0027t run again (unless of course a higher priority task has already added back to the work queue and scheduled it again - in which case we would want to run again)\n\nThis also ensures all access to tcpc_lpm_pending is through an atomic operation (which guarantees the volatile reads and writes)",
      "parentUuid": "721657e1_f7c07cbc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 243,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 244,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c86ee06_c4aaa478",
        "filename": "driver/tcpm/tcpci.c",
        "patchSetId": 6
      },
      "lineNbr": 244,
      "author": {
        "id": 1123814
      },
      "writtenOn": "2018-05-23T00:10:58Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think any of that is necessary here. A bit being set in tcpc_lpm_pending simply means that a port, given by the bit position, has requested to go into low power mode. That indication can only get cleared following the I2CIDLE command being sent. At that point the TCPC is in low power mode and there can\u0027t be any new request to go in to low power mode than can matter. It can only be a duplicate request and so ignoring it is actually the desired outcome.\n\nIf the PD requests entry into low power mode while this function is running in the hook task, allowing that request to persist will result in waking the TCPC from low power mode, because its slave address will be on the I2C bus. Then the TCPC will reset, and the PD task will run again, nothing will be attached, and it will call into drp_toggle again, which will result in this deferred function getting called. It ends up at the same place, but with an extraneous exit/enter taking place. I don\u0027t see how that is better than simply ignoring the duplicate request from the start. The hook call should only be deferred if a low power entry request is pending.",
      "parentUuid": "9ca9c62c_8e7ce39e",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 243,
        "startChar": 1,
        "endLine": 244,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "43da1a7bb83ff37a63d4393524b64aa9a99ded15",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba",
      "unresolved": true
    },
